<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/maui/design"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             mc:Ignorable="d"
             x:Class="Plutus.Frontend.AppClient.Views.MainTill.Settings.SettingsView"
             Title="{Binding Title}"
             IconImageSource="{Binding Icon, Converter={StaticResource MaterialIconConverter}}">
    <!--
        ⚠⚠ ONE COLUMN, since 2026-08-21. Matt: *"Can the settings screen in MAUI be made to look like
        the webtill please, so its consistent."* This was a TWO-COLUMN grid whose sections were dealt
        left/right/left/right by arrival order — so "Printer" could sit beside "Till device" with no
        relationship between them, and the reading order changed with the window width.

        The web till is a single column of collapsible groups, closed by default, "so the page reads as
        a table of contents" (its own comment). This now is too.

        ⚠ The orientation-swapping code-behind went with it: it existed only to fold two columns into
        one on a narrow window. One column needs no such thing, and that is the point — a layout with
        no modes has no mode to get wrong.
    -->
    <ContentPage.Content>
        <ScrollView>
            <!-- ⚠ MaximumWidth, not Fill: settings read badly as full-width lines on a 24" till
                 screen, and the web till's own panel is width-capped for the same reason. -->
            <VerticalStackLayout x:Name="SectionsColumn"
                                 Margin="10,6"
                                 Spacing="0"
                                 MaximumWidthRequest="720"
                                 HorizontalOptions="Start"/>
        </ScrollView>
    </ContentPage.Content>
</ContentPage>
